<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SpotBugs exclude filter for NexusPiercer.

  Every entry below must state WHY it is suppressed. An unexplained suppression is
  indistinguishable from a bug someone hid, and this repository already demonstrates what
  happens when a gate's configuration is never read by a human.

  Do not add blanket <Match><Bug category="..."/></Match> entries. Suppress a specific
  pattern in a specific class, or fix the finding.
-->
<FindBugsFilter
        xmlns="https://github.com/spotbugs/filter/3.0.0"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="https://github.com/spotbugs/filter/3.0.0 https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spotbugs/spotbugs/4.8.3/spotbugs/etc/findbugsfilter.xsd">

    <!-- Generated Avro classes are not ours to fix. -->
    <Match>
        <Source name="~.*[\\/]generated-sources[\\/].*"/>
    </Match>
    <Match>
        <Source name="~.*[\\/]generated-test-sources[\\/].*"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
      DELETED 2026-08-17: the five-class x three-pattern blanket that used to sit here.

      It named AvroReconstructor, GAvroSchemaFlattener, JsonFlattener, JsonReconstructor and
      MapFlattener against UPM_UNCALLED_PRIVATE_METHOD, SIC_INNER_SHOULD_BE_STATIC_ANON and
      RCN_REDUNDANT_NULLCHECK_OF_NONNULL_VALUE, with no <Method> narrowing at all. It masked ten
      real findings in hand-written Java. All ten are now FIXED, so there is nothing left to
      suppress and the block is gone rather than narrowed:

        UPM x5 - five dead private methods deleted. Verdicts established from the occurrence
          count of each symbol in every historical revision of its file:
            AvroReconstructor.reconstructNestedArrayOfRecords  BORN DEAD (never called, ever)
            MapFlattener.flattenSingleValue                    BORN DEAD (never called, ever)
            AvroReconstructor.unwrapUnion                      BORN DEAD (never declared while
                                                               called; never called once declared)
            AvroReconstructor.calculateArraySize               caller replaced by determineArraySize
            GAvroSchemaFlattener.isNullable(Schema)            caller replaced by node.nullable

          CORRECTED 2026-08-17, one revision after the above was first written: unwrapUnion was
          filed as "caller replaced by unwrapNullable" and that is FALSE. Measured with
          `git grep -n unwrapUnion <rev>` at every revision of the file: through ef625f2 there are
          FOUR occurrences and all four are CALLS - there is no declaration in any revision where
          a call exists. The declaration appears for the first time at cad816b, the same commit
          that deleted all four calls, and it never acquired a caller afterwards. The class has no
          superclass and the repo-wide count equals the per-file count at every revision, so it was
          not inherited either. The file was Groovy-compiled at the time (gmavenplus in ef625f2's
          pom), so those four calls compiled and would have thrown MissingMethodException had they
          ever been reached. unwrapUnion is therefore BORN DEAD in the strongest sense of the three
          - it never coexisted with a caller in any revision of the project. The deletion verdict is
          unchanged and in fact safer than originally argued; only the narrative was wrong.

          In the two genuine "replaced" cases - calculateArraySize (declaration at ef625f2:2018 plus
          one call at ef625f2:1192) and isNullable(Schema) - the substitute was installed at the
          same decision point by the same commit, so nothing stopped running and no behaviour was
          restored.

        SIC x3 - three `new TypeReference<List<Object>>() {}` expressions in instance methods
          hoisted into private static final constants, clearing the synthetic this$0 and
          removing a per-parse allocation.

        RCN x2 - two null checks in reconstructNestedRecordFromArray removed, guarding the
          returns of reconstructArrayFromValues and reconstructNestedArrayOfRecordsAtIndex,
          neither of which can return null on any path.

      JsonFlattener and JsonReconstructor never contributed a single finding to this block; they
      were pure collateral and are simply gone from it.

      ON THE COUNT - and this paragraph predicted the wrong number, so read the measurement and
      not the prediction. It said "SpotBugs stays at 241 ... fixing them returns the number to
      241". MEASURED on the committed tree with the CI invocation
      (`grep -o '<BugInstance ' target/spotbugsXml.xml | wc -l`): 237, and 237 is the ceiling
      recorded in .github/quality-baseline.json. The walk is 241 with the block, 251 without it,
      237 after the fixes. It fell by 4 rather than staying flat because four findings that were
      already INSIDE the 241 were carried off by the repairs rather than merely unmasked by them:
      three lived in the body of calculateArraySize and went with the deletion
      (DE_MIGHT_IGNORE and REC_CATCH_EXCEPTION on its empty catch, SF_SWITCH_NO_DEFAULT on its
      default-less switch), and the fourth was the NP_NULL_ON_SOME_PATH on the JsonFlattener
      constructor, fixed in the same commit. The standing gain is unchanged and is the real point:
      a blanket exemption over five of the largest classes in the library no longer exists, so the
      ELEVENTH instance of any of these three patterns gets counted instead of vanishing. PMD
      moved 361 -> 351, having counted the five dead methods inside its total all along.

      Guarded by src/test/java/io/github/pierce/gates/SpotBugsExcludeHasNoBlanketClassBlockTest,
      which fails if a multi-class Match without a Method returns, in either literal or regex
      spelling.
    -->

    <!--
      EI_EXPOSE_REP / EI_EXPOSE_REP2 fire on every builder and config object that stores or
      returns a caller-supplied collection. Defensive-copying all of them would add an
      allocation to the per-record path that the benchmark corpus is specifically designed to
      measure. Suppressed only for the immutable-by-contract config carriers; NOT suppressed
      globally.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Or>
            <Class name="~io\.github\.pierce\.converter\.ConversionConfig.*"/>
            <Class name="~io\.github\.pierce\..*\$.*Builder"/>
        </Or>
        <Or>
            <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/>
            <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
        </Or>
    </Match>

    <!--
      Two accessors on FlattenedField, named individually rather than by package so a future field
      that starts leaking state is not covered by inheritance from this entry.

      properties() returns the internal map ON PURPOSE. A LeafInterceptor annotates a leaf during
      the flatten — that is the whole point of the hook, documented on both types. A defensive copy
      would make every interceptor silently ineffective: the caller mutates a copy, sees no error,
      and gets an unenriched field. That is precisely the failure mode this repository keeps
      finding, so it is the copy that would be the bug here.

      schema() returns the Avro Schema. Schema is mutable via addProp, but it is also interned and
      shared throughout Avro itself; deep-copying one per leaf would cost a parse per field and
      still hand back something the caller could mutate. Callers must not mutate it — stated on
      the accessor.

      The other five findings SpotBugs raised against this package (SE_BAD_FIELD x2,
      UC_USELESS_OBJECT, SE_NO_SERIALVERSIONID) were fixed, not suppressed.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="io.github.pierce.schema.FlattenedField"/>
        <Or>
            <Method name="properties"/>
            <Method name="schema"/>
        </Or>
        <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/>
    </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
